 "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-1259e3e3ae0f43488c7948dca3b35ce8", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-1259e3e3ae0f43488c7948dca3b35ce8", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"answer": "# Full Deliverable \u2014 Design for a Directional-Bias Experiment on Celebrity Parent\u2013Child Retrieval\n\n**Scope statement (explicit):** This is a **design-only** deliverable, consistent with `/workspace/task.json` (`track.md`: *\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"*). No experiment has been run and none will be claimed. The sandbox is offline and credential-free (no network, no `openai`/`anthropic`/`transformers`/`torch`, no API keys; verified by direct inspection). Execution of LLM calls belongs to a separate credentialed environment; this document is the protocol, frozen artifact schema, scoring rules, and pre-registration to be executed there.\n\n---\n\n## 1) Corpus construction protocol\n\n### 1.1 Feasibility check and recruitment procedure (committed order)\n1. **Assemble a ranked candidate pool** in the credentialed environment (network allowed *there*), frozen before any prompt is generated:\n   - Primary: Wikidata SPARQL pre-fetch \u2014 people (`P31=Q5`) who are the `P40` (child) of a parent who is also `P31=Q5`, then filter to pairs where both have a Wikipedia sitelink and a non-trivial article. This yields a large starting pool.\n   - Secondary: Wikipedia category trees (\"Children of actors\", \"Children of musicians\", \"Children of politicians\", \u2026) and curated \"celebrity families\" lists from reputable outlets (used only as *candidates*, never as ground truth \u2014 ground truth must be confirmed by the validation step).\n2. **Stratified target sampling:** from the candidate pool, aim for **N_target = 120** matched families, balanced ~50/50 by **parent gender** (father/mother) and across **four domains** (film/TV, music, sports, politics/royalty) with roughly equal cell fills (e.g., ~30 per domain).\n3. **Interim feasibility gates (pre-committed):** after assembling 60 and 100 qualifying families, check cell balance and exclusion burn-rate. If a stratum is underfilled, oversample it in the next recruitment batch instead of reweighting post hoc.\n4. **Freeze:** once N_target or the candidate universe is exhausted, versions the TSV (`v1.0`) before any model call.\n\n### 1.2 Inclusion/exclusion checklist (applied per candidate pair, all must hold)\n**INCLUDE** requires:\n- [ ] I1. Both `parent_name` and `child_name` have a **standalone Wikipedia/BLP article** (not a redirect-only or disambiguation page) and a Wikidata QID.\n- [ ] I2. The relation is verifiable via Wikidata `P22`/`P25` (father/mother) **and** corroborated by \u22651 independent source beyond Wikidata (biography text, news profile, memoir).\n- [ ] I3. Relationship type is biological or adopted parent\u2013child. (Adoptive allowed only if sources state it unambiguously.)\n- [ ] I4. **Main-set criterion (strong):** the child has **exactly one notable parent** in the pair (the other parent is non-notable/unknown), **and** that parent has **exactly one notable child** in our ground truth. This makes both probe directions strictly **single-target** (perfectly symmetric scoring). Families with two equally famous parents are allowed only in the secondary variant under the lenient \u22651 rule (flagged in the TSV).\n- [ ] I5. Both names are **disambiguation-clean**: no other notable person shares either name (checked against Wikipedia/Disambig lists).\n\n**EXCLUDE** if any of:\n- [ ] E1. Name-disambiguation ambiguity (two notable people share the name).\n- [ ] E2. Recent rename/rebrand or reliance on a stage name with no unambiguous legal-name link.\n- [ ] E3. Gender-identity/pronoun ambiguity that makes a \"parent\"/\"child\" answer ambiguous in the primary generic-wording run.\n- [ ] E4. Contested or legally disputed parentage (sources disagree, or paternity disputed in coverage) \u2014 resolved by the two-source corroboration rule; if sources conflict, exclude.\n- [ ] E5. Either person is primarily notable **for** the family relationship itself with no independent career (this conflates the construct with the bias we measure); the person must have independent notability (own article with substantive content).\n\n### 1.3 Inter-annotator agreement\n- Two independent annotators mark **include/exclude**, the canonical names, and the Wikidata QIDs for every candidate pair.\n- Report **Cohen's \u03ba** on the include/exclude decision. Target \u03ba \u2265 0.80. If \u03ba < 0.70, revise criteria (documented diff) and re-run a fresh sample before continuing.\n- Disagreements go to a third reviewer; per-pair resolution recorded in `curation_notes`.\n\n### 1.4 Fallback / stopping rule (pre-committed)\n- **Minimum usable N = 80** for the primary single-target analysis. \n- Recruitment stops when N=120 is reached **or** the candidate universe is exhausted (defined as: the ranked pool's inclusion candidates are all processed).\n- If 80 \u2264 N < 120: proceed, and report a **post-hoc power calculation at the realized N and realized discordance rate** (exact McNemar; achieved power). \n- If N < 80 but \u2265 40: the multi-child **fanout set** (see \u00a71.5) may be added to reach \u226580, but **only as a labeled coprimary sensitivity subset** \u2014 it is never pooled silently into the primary single-target estimate; the primary claim is then downgraded to \"direction effect observed in single-target families, with reduced power; results inconclusive\" if McNemar `p \u2265 0.05`.\n- If N < 40: declare the primary question inconclusive; report only exploratory estimates.\n\n### 1.5 Secondary set (fanout control)\n- ~40 additional **multi-notable-child** families (same validation rules), scored with **set recall** (`|model \u2229 expected| / |expected|`). Used only to test whether the asymmetry changes with target-set size; kept as a separate table in results, never merged into the primary \u0394\u0304.\n\n---\n\n## 2) Frozen artifact schema + pre-registration\n\n### 2.1 Column-by-column schema (`celebrity_parent_child_v1.0.tsv`, UTF-8, one row per family)\n| # | Column | Type | Definition |\n|---|--------|------|------------|\n| 1 | `family_id` | str | `FAM###` unique id |\n| 2 | `parent_name` | str | Canonical full name of the celebrity parent |\n| 3 | `parent_gender` | enum | `male`/`female` (as reflected in sources for mother/father) |\n| 4 | `parent_qid` | str | Wikidata QID |\n| 5 | `parent_profession` | str | Primary domain tag (`film`/`tv`/`music`/`sports`/`politics`/`royalty`) |\n| 6 | `child_name` | str | Canonical full name of the notable child |\n| 7 | `child_gender` | enum | `male`/`female` |\n| 8 | `child_qid` | str | Wikidata QID |\n| 9 | `child_profession` | str | Domain tag |\n| 10 | `relation` | enum | `biological`/`adoptive` |\n| 11 | `single_target` | bool | `true` (I4 holds) vs `false` (two-famous-parent variant, lenient rule) |\n| 12 | `expected_parents_json` | str | JSON array of target parent canonical names (length 1 in main set) |\n| 13 | `expected_children_json` | str | JSON array of target child canonical names (length 1 in main set) |\n| 14 | `parent_aliases_json` | str | JSON array of aliases (incl. stage/legal names) for entity resolution |\n| 15 | `child_aliases_json` | str | JSON array of child aliases |\n| 16 | `source_refs` | str | `;`-separated URLs/refs (\u22652) |\n| 17 | `annotator1`, `annotator2`, `adjudicator` | enum | include/exclude votes and final |\n| 18 | `curation_notes` | str | Disambiguation/relationship notes |\n| 19 | `date_frozen` | date | Date the v1.0 snapshot froze |\n\n**Worked example row (illustrative, not a real measured datum):**\n```\nfamily_id= FAM001\nparent_name= Jay-Z\nparent_gender= male\nparent_qid= Q941689            # illustrative\nparent_profession= music\nchild_name= Blue Ivy Carter\nchild_gender= female\nchild_qid= Q16733461           # illustrative\nchild_profession= music\nrelation= biological\nsingle_target= true\nexpected_parents_json= [\"Shawn Corey Carter\",\"Jay-Z\"]\nexpected_children_json= [\"Blue Ivy Carter\"]\nparent_aliases_json= [\"Jay-Z\",\"Jay Z\",\"Shawn Carter\",\"Shawn Corey Carter\"]\nchild_aliases_json= [\"Blue Ivy\",\"Blue Ivy Carter\"]\nsource_refs= <ref1>;<ref2>\nannotator1= include; annotator2= include; adjudicator= include\ncuration_notes= child has exactly one notable parent (mother non-notable); single notable child per ground-truth.\ndate_frozen= YYYY-MM-DD\n```\n\n### 2.2 Pre-registration (written before any result is seen; committed verbatim)\n> **Primary question.** For real-world celebrity parent\u2013child relations, is retrieval of the parent given the child more accurate than retrieval of the child given the parent?\n> **Primary metric (pre-specified).** Per-family direction accuracy `acc_C\u2192P(i)` and `acc_P\u2192C(i)` (0/1, single-target main set), family-level difference `\u0394_i = acc_C\u2192P(i) \u2212 acc_P\u2192C(i)`, and the point estimate `\u0394\u0304 = mean_i(\u0394_i)` with a **bootstrap 95% CI** (10,000 resamples, seeded).\n> **Significance threshold.** Two-sided **McNemar\u2019s exact test** on the within-family 2\u00d72 concordance table; the directional-bias claim is supported iff `p < 0.05` **and** `\u0394\u0304 > 0`, **and** the effect direction holds within both parent-gender strata, **and** it survives the anchor-recognition restriction (\u00a74.4).\n> **Strata (pre-specified).** parent gender (male/female); domain (film/TV, music, sports, politics/royalty).\n> **Anchor-recognition condition (pre-specified).** Probes are scored in two ways: (a) \u201call probes\u201d, and (b) \u201crestricted\u201d \u2014 only probes whose anchor was recognized by the model in the control probe (`recognized` if the model affirms the person is real/well-known or otherwise demonstrates recognition; see \u00a74.4). The primary confirmatory analysis is the **restricted** set; the \u201call probes\u201d result is reported alongside.\n> **Confirmed claim.** Directional bias is considered demonstrated only on the primary single-target matched set; all multi-child/fanout, gender-variant, and descriptor-loaded runs are labeled **secondary/exploratory** and cannot by themselves support the claim.\n\n---\n\n## 3) Prompt templates, decoding, and scoring\n\n### 3.1 Prompt templates (primary run, **descriptor-free** anchors)\n- `C\u2192P` (child\u2192parent): \n  `Who is/are the parent(s) of {child}? If the parent is not publicly known, answer exactly: \"I don't know\".`\n- `P\u2192C` (parent\u2192child): \n  `Who is/are the child(ren) of {parent}? List all you can name. If none are publicly known, answer exactly: \"I don't know\".`\n- **Control (anchor recognition):** `Is {anchor} a real, well-known public figure? Answer Yes/No/Not sure.`\n- **Optional secondary variants** (never used for the primary claim): gendered wording (\"father\"/\"mother\"), and anchor descriptor wording (e.g., \"{child} is a singer from the U.S. ...\").\n\n**Anchor descriptor rule (primary):** prompts carry **no biographical descriptor** of the anchor, and must **never mention the other relative or the relationship** anywhere in the instruction. Rationale: any descriptor risks asymmetric prior triggering (a parent-typed descriptor may prime child-retrieval differently than a child-typed descriptor primes parent-retrieval), and any mention of the target leaks the answer. Recognition is measured independently by the control probe instead.\n\n### 3.2 Decoding / sampling protocol (fixed and reported)\n- Primary: `temperature = 0` (deterministic), `top_p = 1`, `max_tokens = 256`, system prompt = \"You are a knowledgeable assistant. Answer using only publicly known facts.\"\n- Replicates: 3 additional runs at `temperature = 0.7` per probe for variance estimation (reported as per-probe agreement, not averaged into the primary point estimate).\n- Model versions pinned with snapshot date (hosted models drift); at least two models (e.g., a frontier GPT-4o-class model and a smaller/cheaper model such as GPT-4o-mini or Claude Sonnet), chosen at execution time in the credentialed environment.\n- Probe order randomized; for each family the two directional probes are placed in randomized relative order across runs; template assignment counterbalanced so wording is not confounded with direction.\n\n### 3.3 Entity-resolution scoring rules\n1. **Normalization:** lowercase, strip punctuation/whitespace/articles (\"the\", \"a\"), expand via the per-person `aliases_json`.\n2. **C\u2192P:** correct iff `normalized(model response)` \u2229 `expected_parents_json` \u2260 \u2205 **and** response is not an abstention. In the single-target main set this is equivalent to exact alias match to the one expected parent.\n3. **P\u2192C (single-target, main set):** correct iff the expected child\u2019s canonical name \u2208 returned set (target-in-set; extra real children returned alongside do **not** penalize, and any extra names are ignored for scoring).\n4. **Abstention:** exact match to `\"I don't know\"` (or semantically equivalent refusal) is scored as `abstain` \u2014 a third outcome, not a wrong answer. \n5. **Two-famous-parent variant (`single_target=false`):** lenient rule \u2014 C\u2192P correct iff \u22651 expected parent is returned and no *wrong* parent is asserted as the sole answer; P\u2192C per earlier rules. Reported separately.\n6. **LLM-as-judge tie-break (secondary, deterministic fallback):** if normalized string resolution is inconclusive (near-miss, paraphrase, or disputed alias), a *different, unbiased* judge model evaluates with a neutral rubric (\"does the response name the person X, by any known name?\"); human adjudication on remaining disputes. Both \"primary-only\" and \"judge-assisted\" accuracy are reported.\n\n**Worked examples (illustrative):**\n- C\u2192P, expect parent `Jay-Z`/`Shawn Corey Carter`. `\"Beyonc\u00e9 and Jay-Z are both famous.\"` \u2192 **correct** (Jay-Z matched; response also names an extra famous adult, non-penalty). `\"I don't know\"` \u2192 **abstain**. `\"Diddy\"` \u2192 **wrong**.\n- P\u2192C, expect child `Blue Ivy Carter`. `\"Blue Ivy\"` \u2192 **correct** (target in set). `\"Blue Ivy and Rumi\"` \u2192 **correct** (target in set; extra non-target ignored). `\"I don't know\"` \u2192 **abstain**. `\"Sir Carter\"` alone \u2192 **wrong** (target absent).\n\n---\n\n## 4) Analysis pipeline sketch (pseudocode)\n\n```\n# load frozen TSV; for each family compute direction outcomes per probe\nfor fam in corpus:\n    out_C->P = score_CtoP(probe_C->P(fam))          # correct | wrong | abstain\n    out_P->C = score_PtoC(probe_P->C(fam))\n    acc_C->P[fam], acc_P->C[fam] = correctness(out_C->P), correctness(out_P->C)\n\n# 2x2 within-family concordance table\ntable = [[n_both_correct, n_CtoP_only],\n         [n_PtoC_only,    n_neither]]\n\n# point estimate + CI\ndelta_bar = mean(acc_C->P - acc_P->C)\nci = bootstrap_95ci(delta_i, n_resamples=10000, seed=2026)   # resample families, not probes\n\n# McNemar exact (two-sided) on the discordant cells\nb, c = table[0][1], table[1][0]\nmcnemar_p = 2 * min(binom_test(min(b,c), n=b+c, p=0.5),\n                    binom_test(max(b,c), n=b+c, p=0.5, alternative='two-sided'))  # scipy binomtest\n\n# confirmatory rule: delta_bar > 0 and mcnemar_p < 0.05\n# strata checks\nfor stratum in [parent_gender, domain]:\n    report delta_bar[stratum], mcnemar_p[stratum]\n\n# anchor-recognition restriction\nrecognized = {}   # from control probes\nreport full-set and restricted-set (probes where anchor recognized) results separately\n\n# refusal sensitivity\nreport 3x2 table: correct/wrong/abstain x direction\naccuracy_conditional_on_answer = correct / (correct + wrong)   # refusals removed\nreport both accuracies; asymmetry must persist on conditional-on-answer set\n\n# confound analysis (mixed model, e.g., R lme4::glmer or Python glmer-equivalent)\nfit  P(correct_ij = 1) ~ direction + parent_gender + domain + fanout +\n                         anchor_recognized + (1|family)\n# report adjusted direction coefficient, OR, and CI; fanout = target-set size (1 in main set, >1 in secondary)\n\n# secondary: multi-child set-recall table (never pooled into primary delta_bar)\n```\n\n**Deliverables of the pipeline:** per-probe CSV; 2\u00d72 table; \u0394\u0304 + bootstrap CI; McNemar p; stratum tables; refusal table; mixed-model summary; secondary set-recall table; achieved-power report at realized N and discordance.\n\n---\n\n## 5) Feasibility / rationale section\n\n- **Why the single-child restriction is the right fanout control.** A parent plausibly has many children, a child usually one (or two) parents; without control, a \"directional bias\" could simply be **answer-set-size bias** (harder to enumerate many than to name one). Restricting the main set to exactly-one-notable-child and exactly-one-notable-parent families makes the two directions *informationally symmetric* (each names exactly one target from a singleton-ish answer space), so any residual asymmetry is genuinely directional.\n- **Why entity-in-set rather than exact-match for P\u2192C.** A parent legitimately has other (non-notable) children; exact-match would punish the model for a reasonable partial enumeration and conflate recall with precision. Target-in-set measures *retrieval of the target child* \u2014 the exact counterpart of naming the target parent on the other side \u2014 and the same leniency is applied in both directions, so it cannot manufacture an asymmetry.\n- **How each confound is addressed:**\n  - *Family fame / base recognition:* within-family paired design (each family is its own control).\n  - *Model prior knowledge:* anchor-recognition control probe + restricted-set primary analysis; mixed-model covariate `anchor_recognized`.\n  - *Name distinctiveness (parent vs. child name base rates):* within-family pairing cancels family-level distinctiveness; a parent/child distinctiveness split test checks interaction with direction; the child in a \"child of famous X\" family is often the less-recognized name, which is exactly the measured phenomenon and is separated from pure mapping difficulty by the anchor-recognition restriction.\n  - *Fanout/set size:* single-target main set (fanout=1) + separate multi-child set with fanout covariate.\n  - *Sampling imbalance:* matched, both-orientations-per-family construction with gender/domain stratification.\n  - *Refusals:* abstention as a separate outcome; asymmetry must survive accuracy-conditional-on-answering.\n  - *Wording/order effects:* descriptor-free prompts, counterbalanced templates, randomized probe order, replicates, pinned model versions.\n- **Assumptions that remain untestable a priori:** (i) that \"both parties have standalone Wikipedia/QID-verified relations\" operationalizes real-world celebrity faithfully; (ii) that hosted-model knowledge is stable across the study window (mitigated, not guaranteed, by version pinning); (iii) that the exclusion criteria (I4's single-target restriction in particular) do not differentially drop the very families where the effect would be weakest/strongest \u2014 this is mitigated by reporting the burn-rate and by the secondary fanout set, but a selection-effect argument cannot be fully pre-empted; (iv) that abstention behavior is symmetric absent bias (tested by the conditional-on-answer analysis, but not guaranteed); (v) statistical power depends on realized discordance, hence the pre-committed minimum-N and achieved-power reporting.\n\n**Bottom line:** the design isolates *direction* by symmetric single-target pairs within each family, pre-registers the metric and threshold (\u0394\u0304 > 0, two-sided exact McNemar p < 0.05, both gender strata, anchor-recognition-restricted set), controls fanout, refusals, distinctiveness, and priors, and explicitly labels all secondary runs as exploratory. It is a protocol requiring a credentialed execution environment; nothing here has been run or claimed as results.", "evidence": "Direct inspection: /workspace contains only task.json; its documents.track.md is inline metadata (\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"), no track.md file exists on disk (find / -name track.md returned nothing), no datasets/topic files, no API keys in env, no openai/anthropic/transformers/torch (imports fail; only requests available; Python 3.14.4, pip 25.1.1). Network is disabled (requests.get('https://www.wikipedia.org') failed within timeout). /home and /tmp contain no supplied data. These constraints are reflected in the design (frozen hand-curated TSV with Wikidata provenance; execution deferred to a credentialed environment). The plan was approved by the researcher before producing this result-stage deliverable.", "assumptions": ["Celebrity = both parent and child have standalone Wikipedia/Wikidata-backed biographies, relation verified via P22/P25 plus \u22651 independent corroborating source.", "Main analysis restricted to single-notable-parent AND single-notable-child families (I4) so both directions are single-target; two-famous-parent and multi-child families are secondary/exploratory variants.", "Target N=120 matched families; minimum usable N=80; fallback is the labeled fanout subset (never silently pooled) plus achieved-power reporting; N<40 is declared inconclusive.", "Decoding: temperature=0 primary with 3 temperature=0.7 replicates for variance; pinned, date-stamped model versions; at least two models.", "Accuracy = alias-normalized entity resolution; C->P target-in-expected-parent-set (exact in the single-target set); P->C target-in-set (lenient, extra non-target children unpenalized); abstention a separate outcome; LLM-as-judge only for tie-breaks.", "Ground truth is a frozen hand-curated TSV (v1.0) built from a Wikidata/Wikipedia candidate pool in a credentialed environment, because the sandbox is offline.", "Primary prompts are descriptor-free to avoid leakage and asymmetric prior priming; recognition measured via a separate control probe.", "Relationship scope = biological/adoptive; step-parents, in-laws, contested relationships, ambiguous names, and persons notable mainly for the kinship are excluded.", "Primary confirmatory analysis is McNemar exact (two-sided p<0.05) plus delta_bar>0, both gender strata, and the anchor-recognition-restricted set; everything else is labeled exploratory."], "uncertainties": ["Whether the directional effect is driven by fanout/set-size rather than pure reverse-mapping difficulty \u2014 controlled by the single-target main set and fanout covariate, but a residual interaction cannot be fully pre-empted.", "Whether 'I don't know' instructions inflate/deflate asymmetry \u2014 handled by the refusal analysis and accuracy-conditional-on-answering, but abstention behavior can't be assumed symmetric a priori.", "Whether the I4 single-target exclusion criterion differentially selects families \u2014 mitigated by burn-rate reporting and the secondary fanout set, not fully pre-emptable.", "Whether date-stamped model pinning fully controls hosted-model drift \u2014 mitigated, not guaranteed.", "Whether enough real families satisfy I4 (exactly one notable parent + exactly one notable child) to reach N_target, and what the realized discordance rate is \u2014 hence pre-committed gates and achieved-power reporting.", "Whether name-distinctiveness differences between parent and child names still interact with direction after within-family matching \u2014 addressed with a distinctiveness split analysis as a robustness check."], "summary": "Deliver complete pre-registered directional-bias experiment design, protocol, schema, scoring, and pipeline", "stage": "result"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-b6096c40ac5847cb8c908825b934db33", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": nu